WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.495
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.495 --> 00:00:07.798
Where's my second case of Lacroix?

00:00:07.798 --> 00:00:12.209
I must have looked baffled
as I delivered $246 worth of

00:00:12.209 --> 00:00:17.260
groceries to an office manager on
Market Street in San Francisco.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.857
I was fully out of breath.

00:00:20.857 --> 00:00:24.696
I just climbed up five flights of stairs,

00:00:24.696 --> 00:00:28.756
carrying a good 40 pounds of groceries and

00:00:28.756 --> 00:00:33.270
what I get is not a thank you,
but an accusation.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.468
Where's my damn Lacroix?

00:00:38.468 --> 00:00:42.758
What she didn't know was that as
I was biking over to her office,

00:00:42.758 --> 00:00:44.990
I almost got into a car accident.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:49.179
The mercedes adjacent to me swerved
into my lane and to dodge him,

00:00:49.179 --> 00:00:50.560
I had to hit the curb.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.742
I was ultimately okay.

00:00:53.742 --> 00:00:59.100
But the unfortunate victim of that
journey was that second case of Lacroix.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:08.012
Had I been seriously injured,
I would have been compensated, nothing.

00:01:08.012 --> 00:01:11.280
Isn't it so
funny in America if you are an employee,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.889
you fully expect your employers to
compensate you in two parts, wages and

00:01:15.889 --> 00:01:20.870
benefits of which one of the largest
parts of that is health care.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:24.746
But if you are a contractor,
like I was at that moment,

00:01:24.746 --> 00:01:27.120
everything goes out the window.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.634
My name is Josue, and

00:01:30.634 --> 00:01:35.770
I've dedicated my career to building
gig platforms at Uber and DoorDash.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.790
In fact,
I lead the driver pricing team at Uber.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:44.889
So how much workers are compensated
is bread and butter to my every day.

00:01:44.889 --> 00:01:49.455
And yet, I hear so
many stories filled with anguish and

00:01:49.455 --> 00:01:52.936
frustration from gig workers every day.

00:01:52.936 --> 00:01:59.085
So to understand this you better,
I signed up to be a Instacart shopper.

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:03.850
I traded my laptop for
a shopping cart and a bike.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.380
You can check out my
cool setup right here.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:13.139
And in this role I realized just how
hard the job was, I wasn't paid nearly

00:02:13.139 --> 00:02:18.421
enough to live in a tier one city like
San Francisco, I was on the hook for

00:02:18.421 --> 00:02:24.080
all performance related expenses, and
no surprise, I had zero benefits.

00:02:26.933 --> 00:02:33.328
And this isn't just a problem for an
Instacart shopper, or a DoorDash driver,

00:02:33.328 --> 00:02:39.450
20% of our economy, our gig workers,
that's 57 million Americans.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:46.338
Tech giants like Google have more
contractors on their payroll than full

00:02:46.338 --> 00:02:47.443
time employees.

00:02:47.443 --> 00:02:52.423
Universities are increasingly hiring more
adjunct professors where they're not

00:02:52.423 --> 00:02:56.420
on tenure track and therefore
don't actually get more benefits.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.876
In California, we have argued back and

00:03:01.876 --> 00:03:06.608
forth on whether contractors
are employees and vice versa.

00:03:06.608 --> 00:03:14.120
And I say, prop 22, 85,
all of these bills are a red herring.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:20.330
Let's solve the real problem here.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:23.310
Let's decouple health care
benefits from employers.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:28.232
Let me say this again, let's decouple
health care benefits from employers.

00:03:28.232 --> 00:03:37.286
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Right now the way our system is,

00:03:37.286 --> 00:03:38.850
is a historical accident.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.085
Wine company, Kaiser steel was astute
enough to realize after World War II when

00:03:45.085 --> 00:03:49.635
the government put in place wage ceilings
because we had an excess of jobs and

00:03:49.635 --> 00:03:54.042
a shortage of workers, that if they
allowed the use of the onsite clinic as

00:03:54.042 --> 00:03:57.060
a benefit that was
considered non inflationary.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:02.370
They double check with the IRS, and
the IRS said, yeah, yes, that makes sense.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:06.190
And so they did that, and
it was a really effective recruiting tool.

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:11.133
And so other employers followed,
and that's why employers pay for

00:04:11.133 --> 00:04:12.440
our health care.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:16.738
And look, this makes sense in 1940s,

00:04:16.738 --> 00:04:20.362
when an American stayed in
one job their whole lives.

00:04:20.362 --> 00:04:25.474
But we're now in the next century,
where a worker changes

00:04:25.474 --> 00:04:30.375
jobs more than five times
before they even turned 30,

00:04:30.375 --> 00:04:33.528
this system no longer makes sense.

00:04:33.528 --> 00:04:37.974
Why should switching
jobs from driving a GM as

00:04:37.974 --> 00:04:41.718
a factory worker tester to driving for

00:04:41.718 --> 00:04:46.990
Uber have anything to do
with your health insurance?

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.432
Let me confess, I'm Canadian.

00:04:50.432 --> 00:04:53.871
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So growing up,

00:04:53.871 --> 00:04:56.370
I never thought much about healthcare.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:01.341
I never had surprise bills,
a ride to the ambulance didn't cost

00:05:01.341 --> 00:05:06.313
me my month's rent, and
everything I needed from medication,

00:05:06.313 --> 00:05:09.920
to surgeries to services,
were all covered.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:16.557
And when you have chronic illnesses
like diabetes, life sustaining drugs,

00:05:16.557 --> 00:05:21.077
like insulin costs less than $12
a pop versus north of $100 here,

00:05:21.077 --> 00:05:26.290
all the while, infant mortality rates
are lower and health expectancy is higher.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:32.073
Just look at how much more
the US spends on health care,

00:05:32.073 --> 00:05:34.585
double, double that of Canada's.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:42.901
This past month, lawmakers in
California had the rare opportunity to

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:48.218
overhaul the California healthcare
system by providing medical coverage for

00:05:48.218 --> 00:05:49.922
every single resident.

00:05:49.922 --> 00:05:55.092
That bill was killed before it
even made it onto the floor

00:05:55.092 --> 00:06:00.827
because critics said that it was
too crazy and too expensive.

00:06:00.827 --> 00:06:02.589
Well, you want to talk expensive,

00:06:02.589 --> 00:06:05.338
let's talk about the current
US healthcare system.

00:06:05.338 --> 00:06:09.602
Every single year,
employers pay $690 billion to pay for

00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:11.898
employees' health insurance,

00:06:11.898 --> 00:06:17.790
while employees still have to pay out
of pocket for co-pays and deductibles.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:21.038
And if you're not in this
employer-employee binary, so if you're

00:06:21.038 --> 00:06:25.182
self-employed, if you're a contractor,
if you're in between jobs, you have to buy

00:06:25.182 --> 00:06:29.046
from a separate exchange, usually at
higher rates because it's assumed you have

00:06:29.046 --> 00:06:32.410
more health defects when you don't
just because your pool is smaller.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:38.904
And on top of all of this, the government
subsidizes, so you as the taxpayers also

00:06:38.904 --> 00:06:44.456
pay into the current high cost low
performing US healthcare system.

00:06:44.456 --> 00:06:50.788
It's scattershot, it's wasteful, and
some would even say, it's crazy.

00:06:50.788 --> 00:06:55.684
The center for medicaid and
medicare found that we could

00:06:55.684 --> 00:07:00.998
reduce health care costs by 13% or
$600 billion a year

00:07:00.998 --> 00:07:06.020
if we decouple health care from employers,
why is that?

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.653
Number one,
it reduces the number of middlemen and

00:07:09.653 --> 00:07:11.879
brokers in this process
all making a margin.

00:07:11.879 --> 00:07:16.582
Number two, we reduce the amount of
administrative costs, of which right now

00:07:16.582 --> 00:07:20.586
the US pays four times that of Canada and
other wealthy countries.

00:07:22.782 --> 00:07:23.640
So what do we do?

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:29.419
Well, there's a lot of really smart
people thinking about this problem, so

00:07:29.419 --> 00:07:32.820
I don't want to be too prescriptive,
but I'll leave you with two models.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:37.855
The first one is the gay model we've
talked about, it's a single payer

00:07:37.855 --> 00:07:42.150
universal health care system that a lot
of progressives in the US are buying for.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:44.171
But similarly,

00:07:44.171 --> 00:07:48.781
conservatives also want to decouple
the health care system from employers.

00:07:48.781 --> 00:07:52.179
They just want to create one
large health care exchange.

00:07:52.179 --> 00:07:56.255
Labor laws would say that if your
employer no longer had to pay for

00:07:56.255 --> 00:08:00.800
your benefits, they need to increase
your wages to stay competitive.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.053
And in that world, what happens?

00:08:05.053 --> 00:08:06.900
Everyone buys from one system.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:08.322
It's transparent, it's open,

00:08:08.322 --> 00:08:11.490
and it doesn't matter what
your worker classification is.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.727
And that's how the Netherlands
does health care.

00:08:13.727 --> 00:08:16.572
And it's actually how we as
Americans think about car insurance.

00:08:19.843 --> 00:08:22.380
You are all future leaders.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.342
In fact, some of you are running
companies right now.

00:08:25.342 --> 00:08:30.270
You want to do right by your workers, but
you also want to maximize your profit.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:34.936
It's a conflict that anyone operating
a business that uses contractors

00:08:34.936 --> 00:08:39.076
will have to face until we decouple
healthcare from employers.

00:08:39.076 --> 00:08:42.652
Because doing so
will not only increase job mobility and

00:08:42.652 --> 00:08:46.154
reduce the burden on businesses and
think about this,

00:08:46.154 --> 00:08:49.670
but ultimately,
it'll spur innovation in America.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:55.233
Right now, if you have a really great
idea, the reason you're not pursuing it

00:08:55.233 --> 00:08:59.796
should not be because you're afraid you're
going to lose your health insurance.

00:08:59.796 --> 00:09:04.490
The current system is creating
friction to entrepreneurship.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:10.416
We're always afraid of the next bubble.

00:09:10.416 --> 00:09:15.293
We had the internet bubble in the 2000s,
the housing and

00:09:15.293 --> 00:09:21.125
credit crisis in 2008,
we're currently in a student debt crisis,

00:09:21.125 --> 00:09:26.190
but what happens when a fifth of
the US economy has a rainy day and

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:28.593
no safety net to fall back on?

00:09:28.593 --> 00:09:33.288
Imagine yourself, or your friends, or your
family, biking your delivering groceries

00:09:33.288 --> 00:09:36.180
and then out of nowhere a car
beat lines you to the curb.

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:39.460
One medical bill,

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:45.220
one emergency room visit will push
you to the brink of financial ruin.

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:50.681
This is the reality for
57 million Americans and

00:09:50.681 --> 00:09:56.250
that number is only increasing while
health outcomes are decreasing.

00:09:56.250 --> 00:10:02.564
Decoupling healthcare from employers
is not crazy, it's common sense.

00:10:02.564 --> 00:10:03.911
Thank you.

00:10:03.911 --> 00:10:07.515
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:07.515 --> 00:10:09.616
[MUSIC]

